---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const speakCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Output forces acquisition', note: 'Speaking is retrieval practice — the memory lesson\'s testing effect, applied to language. You discover your gaps only when production fails. Input builds understanding; output builds the machine that uses it. You need both.' },
    { math: '2 · Shadowing: the fluency gym', note: 'Speak ALONG with native audio, matching rhythm and intonation in real time. It trains mouth muscles, prosody and speed simultaneously — no partner needed, measurable daily.' },
    { math: '3 · Tutors and exchanges', note: 'italki/Preply tutors (cheap, online), language exchange (you trade languages), conversation meetups. The rule: maximise talking TIME — an hour where you speak 40 minutes beats three where you speak 10.' },
    { math: '4 · Speak before you\'re ready', note: 'Perfectionism delays fluency. Errors with feedback are the fastest teachers (Swain\'s output hypothesis: producing language reveals gaps that input alone never exposes). The willingness to sound bad IS the skill.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/intermediate/speaking-and-shadowing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Speaking is the skill learners avoid most and need most: <strong>production forces retrieval,
    exposes gaps, and trains the mouth</strong>. This lesson is the output strategy — shadowing
    for solo practice, tutors and exchanges for real conversation, and the mindset that treats
    errors as data.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="output hypothesis, shadowing, conversation sources, error mindset" widget="equationStep" config={speakCfg} />

  <h2>Conversation tactics that build skill</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prepare topics, don't rehearse scripts:</strong> pre-learn 10 phrases for a topic, then improvise — preparation plus improvisation grows range without becoming recitation.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for corrections strategically:</strong> frequent interruption kills fluency; ask tutors to note errors and review at the end. Fluency practice and accuracy practice are different sessions.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-talk is free practice:</strong> narrate your day internally in the target language. Zero-cost output volume, available everywhere (the memory lesson's retrieval, continuous).</li>
    <li><strong>Track fluency, not perfection:</strong> record a monthly 2-minute monologue on the same topic — the archive shows progress the daily experience hides (the memory lesson's spacing logic, applied to morale).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Swain, "The Output Hypothesis" (1985); Arguelles on shadowing; italki/Preply tutor practice literature.</p>
</TopicPage>
